<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Bessie_Coleman_in_1923.jpg/960px-Bessie_Coleman_in_1923.jpg" alt="Portrait of Bessie Coleman in 1923" class="w-full rounded-xl shadow-md mb-8" loading="lazy" width="960" height="1254" />

<p>In the winter of 1920 a manicurist from the South Side of Chicago was sitting in a French class in the Loop after her shift, learning the vocabulary of a trade she had never practiced in a country she had never seen. She was twenty-seven. She had one term of college behind her, two jobs in front of her, and a plan that sounded, to almost everyone she explained it to, like a joke.</p>

<p>She wanted to fly. Every flying school in the United States had refused her, because she was Black and because she was a woman. So she was going to France, where the world's most respected aviation schools accepted students on the strength of an application, and where the instruction, the paperwork and the exams were all in French. Before anyone would put her behind the controls of a French aeroplane, she had to be able to understand the person teaching her.</p>

<p>Seventeen months after she first started paying for French lessons, and seven months after she landed in Europe, the <strong>Fédération Aéronautique Internationale</strong> issued her an international pilot's license. Her name was <strong>Bessie Coleman</strong>, and she became the first Black woman and the first Native American woman to hold a pilot's license, and the first American of any race or gender to be licensed directly by the FAI instead of applying through the National Aeronautic Association.</p>

<h2>Atlanta, Texas, 1892: The School Four Miles Away</h2>

<p>Elizabeth Coleman was born on 26 January 1892 in Atlanta, Texas, one of thirteen children of George and Susan Coleman, both sharecroppers. When she was two the family moved to Waxahachie, Texas, where they worked land they did not own. The world she was born into was engineered, in law and in custom, to keep her out of every room she would later want to enter. Her county was one over from Paris, Texas, where whites lynched at least nine Black men between 1890 and 1920; her parents' generation was barred from voting by literacy tests and poll taxes, and from railway cars and public facilities by segregation.</p>

<p>She started school at six, in a one-room wooden shack four miles from the house, and walked it. The school was segregated and short of everything: often there was no paper to write on and no pencil to write with. She loved to read and turned out to be an outstanding mathematics student. Every season the routine broke for the cotton harvest, and every season she went back to the rows.</p>

<p>Two events shaped the next ten years. In 1901 her father left the family for Indian Territory, now Oklahoma, looking for better opportunities; his wife and children stayed in Texas. And at twelve, Coleman was accepted on a scholarship to the Missionary Baptist Church School, which meant a real education, at least for a few years. At eighteen she took her savings to the Colored Agricultural and Normal University in Langston, Oklahoma — today Langston University — and completed one term before the money ran out. She went home.</p>

<p>That is the entire formal education of a woman America would later name a street after at O'Hare International Airport: a segregated one-room school, a church school on scholarship, and one term of college.</p>

<h2>Chicago, 1915: A Barbershop, a Chili Parlor, and Other People's War Stories</h2>

<p>At twenty-three she moved to Chicago to live with her brothers. She found work as a manicurist at the White Sox Barber Shop, where the customers included pilots back from the First World War, full of stories about flying. She took a second job managing a chili parlor, and the two incomes had exactly one purpose: to accumulate money she could not otherwise get, for a training she had not yet found a way to buy.</p>

<p>Then came the testing of the premise. American flight schools of the day admitted neither women nor Black people. In 1918 very few American women of any race held a pilot's license, and the ones who did were predominantly white and wealthy. Coleman applied anyway, and was refused everywhere. The refusal was not a rumor she had heard; it was a wall she kept walking into, worked into her savings plan as a fixed obstacle.</p>

<p>Her brother added the sting that pushed her over. He taunted her with the claim that French women were superior to African American women, because French women could fly.</p>

<h2>The Advice That Turned a Dead End into a Language Course</h2>

<p>The way out came from a newspaperman. <strong>Robert S. Abbott</strong>, founder and publisher of the <em>Chicago Defender</em>, told her to train abroad — in France, where Black people had opportunities and flight training had been industrialized by the war. Abbott publicized her search in his paper, and money followed: sponsorship from the <em>Defender</em> and from the banker <strong>Jesse Binga</strong>, plus the savings from the barbershop and the chili parlor.</p>

<p>Note what the advice required of her. A route through France is not a way around language learning; it is a way through it, with the books closed. French flight instruction meant French theory lessons, French mechanical vocabulary, French airfield talk, French examinations and French paperwork. So Coleman did the thing that thousands of learners postpone for years because it is uncomfortable: she enrolled at a language school and started from zero as an adult with a deadline. She took French classes at the <strong>Berlitz Language Schools in the Chicago Loop</strong> while continuing to work.</p>

<p>Nobody was teaching her French for pleasure, and she was not learning it to pass a test. She was learning the specific language of a job she wanted: numbers, directions, parts of an aircraft, instructions shouted over an engine, the words an examiner uses when a student is about to make a mistake. Her grammar was presumably imperfect. Her goal was not.</p>

<h2>Le Crotoy, 1920: Instruction in French, Seven Days a Week</h2>

<p>On 20 November 1920 she sailed from New York for Paris. She enrolled at the <strong>Caudron brothers' school of aviation at Le Crotoy</strong>, on the northern French coast — the school run by Gaston and René Caudron, among the most respected training operations in Europe — to learn to fly in French Nieuport biplanes.</p>

<p>She was the only student of color in her class. The aircraft were unforgiving, by modern standards lethally so: open cockpits, no dual-control safety net worth the name, an engine and airframe that failed, as her biography notes, "frequently, sometimes in the air." During her training a fellow student was killed in a crash — an event she described as a <strong>"terrible shock"</strong> to her nerves. She stayed.</p>

<p>What she had to master is spelled out in a description of the controls she was flying behind:</p>

<blockquote>A steering system that consisted of a vertical stick the thickness of a baseball bat in front of the pilot and a rudder bar under the pilot's feet. — <strong>on the Nieuport biplanes flown at Le Crotoy</strong></blockquote>

<p>Every one of those instructions, every correction shouted over the engine, every word of the theory classes and every warning about the weather arrived in a second language she had begun roughly a year earlier in a classroom in Chicago. Accounts of exactly how long she trained differ — seven months from her arrival is the figure used by the Smithsonian and PBS, with some Texas histories saying ten months of study — but the outcome is not in dispute.</p>

<h2>15 June 1921: License Number Issued in Paris</h2>

<p>On 15 June 1921 the <strong>Fédération Aéronautique Internationale</strong> issued Bessie Coleman an international pilot's license. She was the first Black woman and the first Native American woman to earn one, and the earliest known Black person to hold an international license. Because she had applied directly to the FAI rather than going through the American body, the National Aeronautic Association, she was also the first American of any race or gender to be credentialed that way.</p>

<p>Determined not to be a licensed pilot who could not safely fly, she spent the next two months taking lessons from a French ace near Paris before she sailed home in September 1921. Reporters met her ship. The <em>Air Service News</em> recorded what America was looking at:</p>

<blockquote>A full-fledged aviatrix, the first of her race. — <strong>Air Service News, September 1921</strong></blockquote>

<p>She was invited as a guest of honor to the all-Black musical <em>Shuffle Along</em>, and the whole house stood up for her — including the several hundred white patrons in the orchestra seats.</p>

<h2>What Her French Course Actually Proves</h2>

<p>Strip the drama away and the method is plain enough to copy, and it is the opposite of the advice most learners receive.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>She learned the language of the thing she wanted, not the language in general.</strong> Aircraft, controls, weather, instructions: her vocabulary list was a job description.</li>
  <li><strong>She set a deadline the world would enforce.</strong> A school term in France with fees already paid is a syllabus nobody can quietly abandon, which is exactly why it worked.</li>
  <li><strong>She did not wait to be ready.</strong> Roughly a year of evening classes and one Atlantic crossing separated the barbershop from a French cockpit. Learners routinely spend longer than that deciding which app to use.</li>
  <li><strong>She chose the conditions over the comfort.</strong> Immersion is uncomfortable and it compresses the timeline; a monolingual classroom at home would not have produced a pilot in seven months.</li>
  <li><strong>She studied the language because an institution had closed a door, not because it was open.</strong> French was not a detour from her goal. It was the only remaining route to it.</li>
</ul>

<p>Anyone learning a language to work abroad, to qualify in a profession whose exams are in another language, or to reach family and community they have been separated from, is doing her exact task: converting grammar drills into a key.</p>

<h2>Queen Bess, 1922 to 1926</h2>

<p>Back in the United States, Coleman discovered there was no commercial flying career waiting for a Black woman, so she invented one. She became a barnstorming stunt pilot — the era's air shows were the only paying market — and immediately found that she could not get advanced instruction at home either. In February 1922 she sailed for Europe a second time, completing an advanced aviation course in France, visiting Anthony Fokker in the Netherlands, and training in Germany with a chief pilot of the Fokker company.</p>

<p>Her American debut came on 3 September 1922 at Curtiss Field on Long Island, at an event honoring veterans of the all-Black 369th Infantry Regiment, sponsored by Abbott and the <em>Chicago Defender</em>, which billed her as "the world's greatest woman flier." Six weeks later she performed in Chicago for the 370th Infantry: figure eights, loops, and near-ground dips in front of a large crowd.</p>

<p>On 4 February 1923, at Santa Monica, her engine stalled just after takeoff and the aircraft went into the ground. She survived with a broken leg and fractured ribs — and asked the doctors to "patch her up" in time for the air show she had organized that week. They kept her grounded for months instead.</p>

<p>She spent the years after that touring, speaking and teaching by example. She refused, absolutely, to appear at aviation events that barred Black attendees. In Orlando she stayed with the Reverend Hezakiah Hill and his wife Viola, opened a beauty shop to raise money for her own aircraft, and kept working toward the goal that had organized her whole life: a flying school for Black pilots. She was offered a role in a feature film, <em>Shadow and Sunshine</em>, and walked off the set when she learned the first scene required her to appear in rags with a stick and a bundle on her back. Doris Rich's biography records the reasoning:</p>

<blockquote>Clearly ... [Bessie's] walking off the movie set was a statement of principle. Opportunist though she was about her career, she was never an opportunist about race. She had no intention of perpetuating the derogatory image most whites had of most blacks. — <strong>Doris L. Rich</strong></blockquote>

<h2>Jacksonville, 30 April 1926</h2>

<p>She died doing what the licenses and the air shows were for. In April 1926 she was in Jacksonville, Florida, preparing for an air show, having just bought a Curtiss JN-4 "Jenny" in Dallas. Her mechanic and publicity agent, twenty-four-year-old William D. Wills, flew it down and had to make three forced landings on the way because the aircraft had been so badly maintained. Friends and family begged her not to fly it. She flew it anyway, in the second seat, unharnessed because she needed to lean out and study the ground for a parachute jump planned for the following day.</p>

<p>About ten minutes into the flight the aircraft went into a dive and then a spin. She was thrown from the cockpit and fell to her death. Wills could not regain control; the plane hit the ground, exploded, and burned. In the wreckage investigators found the cause: a wrench left in the works had jammed the controls. Bessie Coleman was thirty-four.</p>

<p>Ten thousand mourners filed past her coffin in Chicago. The service was presided over by <strong>Ida B. Wells</strong>. The <em>Dallas Express</em>, the South's oldest Black newspaper, wrote in its editorial:</p>

<blockquote>There is reason to believe that the general public did not completely sense the size of her contribution to the achievements of the race as such. — <strong>Dallas Express, 1926</strong></blockquote>

<h2>The School She Never Opened</h2>

<p>The flying school for Black pilots stayed unbuilt, but the students came anyway. Lieutenant William J. Powell, who founded the Bessie Coleman Aero Club in Chicago in 1929, wrote the dedication that explains why a language learner should know this story at all:</p>

<blockquote>Because of Bessie Coleman, we have overcome that which was worse than racial barriers. We have overcome the barriers within ourselves and dared to dream. — <strong>William J. Powell, Black Wings, 1934</strong></blockquote>

<p>Her line runs straight into American aerospace: John Robinson, Cornelius Coffey, Willa Brown, Janet Harmon Bragg, Robert H. Lawrence Jr., and Mae Jemison, who carried a picture of Coleman with her into orbit. Chicago named a public library after her in 1993 and a street at O'Hare; the United States issued a 32-cent stamp in 1995; she entered the National Women's Hall of Fame in 2001 and the National Aviation Hall of Fame in 2006. A mountain on Pluto — Coleman Mons, on the edge of the heart-shaped Tombaugh Regio — carries her name. The United States Mint put her on the American Women quarter in 2023, and in 2025 the City of Brussels named a street beside its airport after her.</p>

<p>What she left behind for a language learner is smaller and more usable than a monument. Every year thousands of people are kept out of a profession, a country, a qualification or a community by paperwork and prejudice they did not create. Coleman's answer was not to argue with the door. It was to find out which language the next door was written in, learn enough of it to be taught, and get on a boat.</p>

